The importance of these moieties is even more evident, thanks to the isotopic exchange experiment between ${\sf H}_{2}$ and ${\sf D}_{2}$ . When exchanging ${\sf H}_{2}$ for ${\sf D}_{2}$ , the signals of the formate groups shifted to lower wavenumbers; the signals in the 2950–2850 cm−1 region shifted to the region of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ roto-vibrational profile and are therefore not detectable. Conversely, the band at $2745\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ shifted to $2168\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\mathsf{39}$ (Figure S16) and the intensity vastly increased as compared to the H-analogue $(\mathsf{I}_{\mathsf{H C O O}}/\mathsf{I}_{\mathsf{D C O O}}\approx\mathsf{0}.02)$ . The intensity increase indicates an inverse kinetic isotope effect, although possible differences in the extinction coefficient of the two species cannot be excluded.  Importantly, a corresponding, gradual increase in methanol production rate was observed upon ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf D}_{2}$ exchange under steady-state ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation conditions (Figure S14). At isotope equilibration, this difference in $H-$ and D-methanol production rates corresponds to an inverse Kinetic Isotope Effect (KIE), $\mathsf{r}_{\mathsf{H}}/\mathsf{r}_{\mathsf{D}}=0.36$ . Moreover, the temporal scale of exchange was very similar for the formate species and for methanol in the parallel ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf D}_{2}$ exchange experiments performed in the FT-IR transmission cell and the test-setup, respectively (Figure 3) (See Experimental section for details). Together, these experiments provide firm evidence that the Zr-formate species is a key intermediate in the methanol formation path.
